# Per-project REVIEW CONTROLLER settings. Copy to projects/<name>/review.env
# — it is gitignored (see projects/* in .gitignore).
#
# Read AFTER projects/<name>/symphony.env and passed to compose via
# --env-file only — this file is NEVER named by an `env_file:` directive
# anywhere in this stack. That is the mechanism that keeps this token out of
# BOTH agent containers (opencode and opencode-review), not a filing
# convention: opencode's env_file: layer is .env + projects/<name>/.env,
# opencode-review's is .env alone, and this value is in neither.
#
# This file is also never mounted into the symphony-review container itself
# — only projects/<name>/config/ is (read-only, as REVIEW.md). `symphony
# check` refuses to start if an agent env file is readable from inside that
# mount, and the reverse holds here by the same logic: this credential file
# has no path into it either.
#
# Same parser rule as symphony.env: comments on their OWN lines, never
# inline after a value.
#
# Read the README's "The security model" first, especially "Three
# credentials" and the reasoned exception below.

# === The review controller's GitLab credential ===============================
# A GROUP access token, REPORTER role, `api` scope — deliberately WIDER in
# reach than the implementation orchestrator's per-project token, and that is
# a considered exception, not a quietly bent rule. Read this before you
# reach for a narrower one and find it doesn't fit:
#
# WHY GROUP-SCOPED IS THE RIGHT CALL HERE. A project access token reaches one
# project. A fleet reviewer watches many — the whole point of the feature is
# coverage across a group, not one repository at a time (see
# templates/REVIEW.md.example's `review.projects` / `review.group_id`).
# Thirty projects would mean thirty tokens, and thirty tokens to rotate is
# not a security posture anyone actually keeps up with; the token that
# nobody rotates because rotating it is too much work is worse than the
# token whose reach is a little wider. One group token, scoped to a group
# created to hold exactly the projects being reviewed (not the organisation
# root), is the narrower choice once "narrow" is measured honestly.
#
# WHY IT IS STILL SAFE. The README's warning against a broad token is a
# warning against a token that can PUSH — a personal access token carries
# your whole identity, and nothing downstream can claw that back. This token
# cannot push, merge, or approve anywhere, in any project, in the group:
# Reporter is read-plus-comment, full stop. And unlike every other credential
# this launcher hands to a container, this one is held ONLY by symphony-review
# — trusted, non-agentic code — and NEVER by the reviewing agent inside
# opencode-review, which has no GitLab access of any kind (see
# docker/docker-compose.review.yml). Total compromise of this token's blast
# radius is comment spam across the group: annoying, fully attributable,
# instantly revocable by rotating one token — not code reaching a default
# branch. Compare the agent's own Developer token (GITLAB_PAT, in
# projects/<name>/.env), whose blast radius on compromise is pushed code on
# one project: this token trades project-scoping for role-scoping, in a
# position where role is the constraint that actually matters, because
# nothing holding it can act beyond what Reporter allows regardless of how
# many projects it can see.
#
# Server-side and free, same as the other two tokens: protect default
# branches, and grant Reporter rather than anything higher so this token
# cannot change project settings even by accident.
#
# Leave this commented out to inherit nothing — review is OFF for this
# project until a token is set. `symphony check` is explicit about whether
# review is on or off; it is never silent either way.
# SYMPHONY_REVIEW_GITLAB_TOKEN=

# === The optional shallow checkout ============================================
# SYMPHONY_REVIEW_CHECKOUT — a behaviour switch, not a credential (it carries
# no secret, unlike the token above; docker-compose.review.yml reads it into
# the symphony-review service's own environment: block the same way it reads
# SYMPHONY_LOG_LEVEL). `1` turns it on; leave it commented out, or set it to
# anything else, and it stays off.
#
# WHAT IT DOES. With it on, the review controller additionally clones the
# merge request's repository, shallow, at the EXACT pinned head commit, before
# handing the review agent its material — giving the agent repository-wide
# context beyond the changed files themselves (the caller of a function whose
# signature changed, a test file that was not touched, an interface a new
# class claims to implement). Without it, the agent sees only the diff and
# the changed files' own contents, exactly as before. Either way the sandbox
# invariant holds: `.git` is deleted, and its removal verified, before the
# review agent's session is ever created (symphony-queue's
# src/review/checkout.ts) — the agent gets a plain directory of files or,
# if anything about the checkout goes wrong, no checkout at all. A checkout
# is a bonus for finding quality, never a prerequisite: a review proceeds
# without one exactly as it always has.
#
# THE HONEST COST, and why this defaults off. A shallow clone per review is
# real wall-clock (a network fetch to GitLab, however shallow, on every
# review run) and real disk (a second, temporary copy of the repository's
# working tree alongside the diff and file blobs symphony-review already
# writes per job). On a large repository, or a project reviewed often, that
# adds up in a way "off by default, opt in per project" is the honest answer
# to. Turn it on for a project where the extra context is worth paying for;
# leave it off elsewhere.
# SYMPHONY_REVIEW_CHECKOUT=1
